---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { digits, pct, usd } from "../../lib/massppe/format";
// What "the same fiscal effort" means, worked through for two towns with one house each and a school they
// share. It was a window on the cap tab, whose "?" beside "fiscal effort" links here (cap.ts, helpLink).
// Fixed numbers, so the page is built whole; Tim will revise it.
const house = 400_000;
const incomes = [100_000, 1_000_000];
const school = 10_000;
// Chapter 70 sets its two percentages so that property and income each yield half the total
const pEx = school / 2 / (2 * house);
const iEx = school / 2 / (incomes[0] + incomes[1]);
const cey = incomes.map((inc) => house * pEx + inc * iEx);

// As the cap tab writes them (format.ts, cap.ts): the effort percentages to three decimals, as DESE gives them
const rate = (v: number) => `${digits(v * 100, 3)}%`;

const rows: { label: string; cells: string[]; total?: boolean }[] = [
  { label: "House, assessed", cells: [usd(house), usd(house)] },
  { label: "Family income", cells: incomes.map((v) => usd(v)) },
  { label: `From property, ${rate(pEx)}`, cells: [usd(house * pEx), usd(house * pEx)] },
  { label: `From income, ${rate(iEx)}`, cells: incomes.map((inc) => usd(inc * iEx)) },
  { label: "Combined effort yield", cells: cey.map((v) => usd(v)), total: true },
  { label: "Share of family income", cells: cey.map((c, k) => pct(c / incomes[k], 1)) },
  { label: "Property tax rate to raise it", cells: cey.map((c) => `$${((c / house) * 1000).toFixed(2)} per $1,000`) },
];
---

<Layout title="Same fiscal effort, two towns - Tim Shores"
  description="What the same fiscal effort means in Chapter 70, worked through for two towns">
  <p class="back"><a href="/muni-spending/?view=cap">The 82.5% cap</a></p>
  <h1>Same fiscal effort, two towns</h1>

  <p>
    Picture two towns, each with one house assessed at {usd(house)}. In Town A, the family earns {usd(incomes[0])} a
    year. In Town B, the family earns {usd(incomes[1])}. The two towns share a school that costs {usd(school)} a year.
    What should each town pay?
  </p>
  <p>
    Chapter 70 sets its two percentages so that property and income each yield half the total. Here that's
    {usd(school / 2)} from {usd(2 * house)} of property, or {rate(pEx)}, and {usd(school / 2)} from
    {usd(incomes[0] + incomes[1])} of income, or {rate(iEx)}. Both towns pay those same two percentages: the same
    fiscal effort.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th class="num">Town A</th><th class="num">Town B</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <tr class={r.total ? "total" : undefined}>
          <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
          {r.cells.map((c) => <td class="num">{c}</td>)}
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Town B pays more because its family earns ten times as much. But the same fiscal effort isn't the same share of
    each family's income. The property half falls on both houses equally, so Town A's family pays
    {pct(cey[0] / incomes[0], 1)} of its income and Town B's pays {pct(cey[1] / incomes[1], 1)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chapter 70 doesn't say how a town raises its share, but Massachusetts towns can't tax income. Raising it with the
    property tax, Town B would tax an identical house at more than twice Town A's rate. The formula expects the town
    with more income to tax the same house harder.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the formula counted property alone, both houses would owe {usd(school / 2)}: {pct(school / 2 / incomes[0], 1)}
    of Town A's family income and {pct(school / 2 / incomes[1], 1)} of Town B's. Counting income moves
    {usd(school / 2 - cey[0])} of the bill from Town A to Town B.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .back a::before {
    content: "← ";
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  tbody th {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .num {
    text-align: right;
  }

  .total th,
  .total td {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
